    <item>
      <headword id="d0e631" type="heading" level="h2">(a) Fertilisers and fertilising</headword>
    </item>
    <item>
      <headword id="d0e637" type="heading" level="h3">1. Fertilisers</headword>
    </item>
    <item>
      <headword id="idp115845152">[“gooana”]</headword>
      <description>in my young days all artificial fertilisers were collectively termed “gooana” (guano). Seldom used.</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <headword id="idp115846664">feamin dhearg</headword>
      <description>red ware washed up on beaches. Each part of the shore was divided between every crofter. “Latha rinn na feamin” was a fertile source of bickering and the occasional fight!</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <headword id="idp115848392">feamin dubh</headword>
      <description>the bladder and other types of seaweed which was cut with “corrans” and carried up in creels. Frequently went with boats to outlying islands to cut this ware. Always used for potato manure. Many boats lost through overloading.</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <headword id="idp115849904">“laigadal”</headword>
      <description>the red tangle with long red fronds only obtained at spring tides – ( traigh “laingadal” [sic]). Generally used for turnip manure.</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <headword id="idp115851416">inneir</headword>
      <description>dung (horse, cow or sheep dung).</description>
    </item>
